# orangery-probe — the orangery on its own, in FACILITY coordinates, so it can be measured without
# sampling the rest of the estate.
#
#   .uildin\WorldShaper.exe --clip-file clipsacilityequests\orangery-probe.clip #        --clip-metre 16 --clip-part part_orangery
#
# and THE CONTROL ARM, which is the whole point of the file:
#
#   .uildin\WorldShaper.exe --clip-file clips\estate\orangery.clip #        --clip-metre 16 --clip-part part_orangery
#
# `clips/facility/orangery.clip` is a copy of `clips/estate/orangery.clip` carried to (42.30, 0.90, -5.25), and the
# estate original is UNTOUCHED — so the estate clip is the control, and the question this probe
# answers is whether the copy is the same clip in a different place or a different clip.
#
# THIS BUILDING CANNOT BE COMPARED TO THE VOXEL, AND THE MODULE IS WHY.
#
# D672's control-arm method rests on the translate being a whole number of cells, so that both arms
# put the building on the same grid and one voxel of difference is a fault. **That does not hold
# here at any resolution.** The vector is (42.3, 0.9, -5.25), and:
#
#     42.3 x 8 = 338.4     x 16 = 676.8     x 32 = 1353.6
#
# none of them whole. It is not a bad choice of number, it is the estate's own module: the site plan
# is laid out on 0.45 m, and **0.45 x 32 = 14.4**, so ANY module-derived offset is off the voxel grid
# at every resolution the game uses. The four buildings D672 converted compare exactly because their
# vectors happen to be whole METRES -- (30, 0, 14), (-34, 0, 22), (-36, 0, 16), (0, 0, 46) -- which
# is an accident of the site plan and not a property of the method.
#
# So `volume` and `surface` WILL differ between the arms by a few thousand voxels of resampling, and
# that difference is not a fault and cannot be tuned away. Measured, metre 8, fountain:
# **506,728 against the control's 500,333** and 151,902 faces against 151,244. What is still worth
# reading is `never fired`, the material SET, and the component count -- none of which depends on
# the grid.
#
# To get an exact arm somebody would have to move the building to a whole number of voxels, which
# changes the site plan, or compare at a resolution where the module divides, which does not exist.
# Neither is this file's decision to make.
#
# WHAT THE BOX COSTS, 60.5 x 13.0 x 13.0 m, because a probe that will not run is not an instrument:
#
#   metre  8     5.2 M cells    0.03 GB
#   metre 16    41.9 M cells    0.21 GB   THE DEFAULT BELOW
#   metre 32   335.0 M cells    1.68 GB   runs, and is the metre the clip is authored at
#
# THE TRANSLATE HAS A HEIGHT IN IT AND THIS IS THE ONLY ONE THAT DOES: (42.30, 0.90, -5.25). The
# orangery stands on the east terrace rather than on the lawn. At metre 16 a cell is 0.0625 m and
# 0.90 is a whole number of them, so the two arms still land on the same grid and must agree to the
# voxel; at metre 8 (0.125 m) 0.90 is 7.2 cells and THEY WILL NOT -- so compare at 16, not at 8.
#
# ITS EAST END IS 0.50 m FROM THE MANIFEST'S OWN `bounds` HIGH of x 72.5. This probe's box is cut to
# 72.5 for that reason: it is where the building stops being sampled in the real world, so a probe
# that reached further would measure matter the game never builds.
#
# THE THREE THINGS TO READ, in the order they catch things:
#
#   never fired   a rule that painted nothing. Silent, and it looks exactly like success — it is the
#                 first thing a botched translate shows. If the geometry was carried and a shape a
#                 rule names was not, the rule asks its question where the building is not and
#                 paints nothing. NEITHER run should print this line at all.
#   materials     the same set. The weathering coats will not match the control exactly and are not
#                 meant to: a weather mask is noise asked in WORLD coordinates, so carrying the
#                 building slides it across the field. The PAINTS have no such excuse.
#   components    the same count as the control, whatever that count is. It is not 1 and is not
#                 meant to be; what matters is that the two arms agree.
#
# AND THE SHARES WILL NOT MATCH EXACTLY, for two reasons that are both deliberate. The estate clip
# carries its own base coat and this probe uses the manifest's; and every rule in the fragment is
# `below=0.035` rather than 0.02, which lets each rule reach further and hands surface from the
# first rule to the ones after it. Both are argued in clips/facility/orangery.clip's own header.
#
# NOT YET RUN. This file is written from the conversion's arithmetic and has not been executed
# against either arm — see the decision log entry that added the three fragments. Whoever runs it
# first should replace this paragraph with the numbers, the way campanile-probe.clip does.

include "../_contract.clip"
include "../_order.clip"

# The coat everything starts in, exactly as the manifest has it.
paint limestone

include "../orangery.clip"

# `metre` and `bounds` come AFTER EVERY include and not before -- `_order.clip` includes
# `_contract.clip` again and the contract re-states the estate's box and its metre, so these two
# lines put anywhere above the last include are quietly undone by it, and the undoing looks exactly
# like the narrowing having had no effect. Written out in ballroom-probe.clip, which paid for it.
metre 16
bounds 12 -1 -12.5   72.5 12 0.5

solid part_orangery
